
# discovery

## resilience
- label: a session tree that discovery cannot fully read still yields every session it CAN read: an unreadable directory (EACCES/EPERM), a symlink cycle (ELOOP), a directory that vanishes between the parent readdir and the nested one (ENOENT), and a path that turns out not to be a directory (ENOTDIR) are each skipped as that one subtree, never propagated out of findSessions — so one 0700 folder or one self-referential symlink under any discovery root can no longer empty the entire dashboard
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/discovery-resilience.test.js
  tags: [session-stability, discovery, spec, implemented]
- label: the JS discovery walk bounds itself: every recursive descent carries a depth cap and a visited real-path guard, so a symlink that points at one of its own ancestors terminates instead of recursing until ELOOP or a stack overflow, and a directory reachable by two paths contributes its sessions exactly once
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'symlink|cycle|depth' test/sessions/discovery-resilience.test.js
  tags: [session-stability, discovery, spec, implemented]

## caches
- label: OpenCode discovery keeps at most one cached row set per database: the cache is keyed by database path and holds only the newest mtime's rows, so a long-running serve against an actively-written opencode.db reuses one slot instead of accumulating a fresh array of up to 10000 session rows for every mtime the database has ever had
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'opencode discovery cache' test/sessions/discovery-resilience.test.js
  tags: [spec, session-stability, discovery, implemented]

# index

## durability
- label: a tracequest command that cannot persist its caches still exits 0: the exit-path flush guards the search.idx write exactly as it already guards the index.json write, so an unwritable or vanished ~/.cache/tracequest makes the next run rebuild rather than making this run die with an uncaught EACCES stack trace after its work already succeeded
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'exit flush' test/sessions/index-exit-flush.test.js
  tags: [session-stability, index, spec, implemented]

# watch

## coverage
- label: every root discovery scans is a root the server watches: defaultDataRoots is derived from the same layout the discovery roots come from, so the cursor-cloud import root and each ssh-imported host tree raise data-update ticks like a local root does, and an imported session appears in the dashboard without a restart
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'data roots cover|imported host|cursor-cloud' test/server/data-root-coverage.test.js
  tags: [session-stability, fs-watch, spec, implemented]

## polling
- label: the live-session endpoint walks the session tree ONCE per request, not twice: /api/sessions/live resolves a hash handle and computes liveness from a single findSessions result instead of calling it again for each, so an open live chat page — which polls once a second and bypasses the route cache by design — costs one tree walk per poll rather than two
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 --test-name-pattern 'single scan|one discovery scan' test/routes/session-live-routes.test.js
  tags: [spec, session-stability, api, implemented]

# live

## degraded-probes
- label: a process probe that FAILS is not evidence that nothing is running: when a pgrep invocation times out or errors, detectLiveSessions carries the previous snapshot's still-existing live paths forward instead of reporting an empty set, so a loaded machine — where a 2s-capped probe routinely times out — no longer makes every running agent blink out of the dashboard and back on the next poll; a probe that succeeds and matches nothing still means nothing is running, and the carried-forward set is dropped as soon as a clean pass replaces it
  command: TRACEQUEST_SKIP_LR_WATCH=1 node --test --test-concurrency=1 test/sessions/live-probe-degraded.test.js
  tags: [spec, session-stability, live, implemented]

## probe-latency
- live detection answers from ONE process-table snapshot per pass rather than one pgrep spawn per binary per match-form: the exact-name and full-argv match semantics stay exactly as live-sessions-platform.facts describes them, but they are evaluated against a single enumeration instead of nine serial synchronous spawns, so a detection pass on a busy host costs one traversal rather than nine and stops blocking the event loop for seconds at a time @draft @session-stability @live @needs-decision
